Document (dev.stxt.website): Design principles | STXT

	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-16
		Description: The design principles of STXT: a language designed for people first. Simplicity, indentation as structure and parsing safety all derive from that principle.

	Header: Design principles

	Assert >>
		@STXT@ is a language designed for people (**Human-First**). Every design and
		construction decision of the language derives from that principle.

	Subheader: 1. The document is written for people

	Content >>
		This is the principle the others follow from. An @STXT@ document must be readable and
		writable without tools. Hence there are no escape characters, braces, brackets,
		mandatory quotes or closing tags, and a document looks like an outline.

		When a design question arises, this principle is applied to settle it.

	Subheader: 2. KISS, simplicity above all

	Content >>
		@STXT@ applies the KISS principle (*keep it simple*) on every plane at once:

		* **Simple to write and read.** There are few rules: indentation,
		  two kinds of node (`Name: value` or `Name >>`), optional namespaces and comments.
		  There are no escape characters, lists or shorthand notation.
		* **Simple to implement.** A conforming parser is a line-by-line pass with a
		  stack. Schemas and validation are separate, optional layers.

	Subheader: 3. Safety from the design

	Content >>
		@STXT@ applies safety in the design decisions themselves and not as a later
		add-on. Hence, for example, the absence of features
		or the restriction of namespace characters to alphanumeric ASCII.
